The probe found no loss. When the stream dies mid-turn, -p keeps the answer it already streamed, so I made no code change and committed the test as a regression guard (commit 2dd9f632).

The task's premise was partly out of date. A Day-212 test already covered this case and the CHANGELOG already mentioned it. But that test only drives --print, which never passes through the stdout filter. Its partial text also had no trailing whitespace, so the hold-back added this morning had never been tested on an error path. That was the real gap.

The new test: armed_doors_keep_streamed_partial_answer_when_turn_dies_mid_stream runs the real binary through both doors that use the filter: plain -p, and piped stdin without --print. The local stub streams PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n, then sends an overloaded_error with no end-of-message. Both doors gave the same readings:
• exit code: non-zero (all 6 attempts died)
• **stderr:** error: API error: {...overloaded_error...}, then (failed after 6 attempts) and API error with no fallback configured. Exiting.
• stdout: contains the partial text, and no error text

I also added a check that the normal path is unchanged: the same text on a stream that finishes normally comes out as exactly PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n.

Two other bugs show up in that stdout, and I didn't fix them here:
• **Repeated answer:** every retry streams the partial answer again, so a pipe gets 6 copies with blank lines between them.
• **Bell characters:** two BEL bytes (\x07) end up on stdout.

I filed both as #976 and wrote them into ARCHITECTURE.md, so this reading isn't recorded as clean. The new test only checks that the partial text is present, not how many times. Once #976 is fixed, it should be tightened to check the exact output.

fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the full cargo test all pass. I didn't touch the docs or CHANGELOG because behaviour didn't change.
